.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAM-REVOKE-ROLE 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eam-revoke-role \- remove one grant
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eam revoke-role
.BR \-\-grant\-id | \-g
.I ID
.SH DESCRIPTION
Removes one grant, taking away whatever it gave.
.SH BY ID, NOT BY ROLE AND PRINCIPAL
The same role may be granted to the same principal more than once with
different scope \-
.I reader
in
.IR staging ,
.I reader
in
.I production
over two named topics \- and revoking has to say which. A grant's scope
is fixed once written, so granting twice is the normal way to express two
scopes rather than a mistake, and role-and-principal would not identify
one of them.
.PP
.BR euclid-cli-eam-grant-role (1)
prints the id when it creates the grant.
.B euclid-cli eam list-grants --principal <ern>
shows the ids of everything one principal holds, and
.B --role <name>
of everything that grants one role.
.SH NARROWING A GRANT
There is no way to edit one. To take one namespace away from a grant that
names three, revoke it and grant the remaining two \- the grant gets a
new id, and anything holding the old one should look it up again.
.SH AN ID THAT NAMES NOTHING IS NOT AN ERROR
Revoking a grant that has already been revoked succeeds. The call says
what should be true afterwards \- that this grant does not exist \- and
that is true either way, so a script that revokes a list can be re-run
after failing halfway through.
.PP
The consequence to know about: a mistyped id also succeeds, and nothing
was removed. Check with
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-grants (1)
rather than with the exit status.
.SH WHAT DOES NOT GO AWAY WITH IT
The role. Revoking the last grant of a role leaves the role in place, to
be granted again \-
.BR euclid-cli-eam-delete-role (1)
is what removes it, and it refuses while any grant still names it.
.PP
Group membership. A user who holds something through a group keeps it
until that group's grant is revoked or the user leaves the group \-
.BR euclid-cli-eam-user-group-remove-user (1).
.BR euclid-cli-eam-check-permission (1)
answers the combined question.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-grant\-id " " \fIID\fR
The grant's id, as
.B grant-role
and
.B list-grants
report it. Required.
.SH WHO MAY RUN IT
Installation administrators \- members of the
.I administrator
user group. See
.B ADMINISTRATOR PRIVILEGES
in
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The grant does not exist any more \- whether this call removed it or it
was already gone.
.TP
.B 1
No id given, the request could not be sent, or the caller is not an
administrator. If the failure was an expired or invalid session
(\fIHTTP 401\fR), the stored credentials file is removed; run
.B euclid-cli eam login
again before retrying.
.SH EXAMPLES
Find it, then remove it:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam list-grants \e
    --principal ern:eam:eu-central-1:000000000000:user:jdoe

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam revoke-role \e
    --grant-id 7c1f2f5e-3a21-4e6b-9a0c-5f2b9e1d4c33
.fi
.RE
.PP
Take one namespace away from a grant that covered two:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam revoke-role \e
    --grant-id 7c1f2f5e-3a21-4e6b-9a0c-5f2b9e1d4c33

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam grant-role \e
    --role publisher \e
    --principal ern:eam:eu-central-1:000000000000:user:jdoe \e
    --namespace staging
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-grant-role (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-grants (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-check-permission (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-delete-role (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-user-group-remove-user (1)
